"""General US legal-industry terms for the Glossary, written from general knowledge of US law and practice (not from Tettra).
Plain-English meanings; rules differ by state and court, so each one is a starting point. (term, category, definition[, example])
Categories: Legal, Litigation, Personal Injury, Insurance & Liens, Medical-Legal."""
L, T, P, I, M = 'Legal', 'Litigation', 'Personal Injury', 'Insurance & Liens', 'Medical-Legal'
TERMS = [
# ---- Legal basics
('Affidavit', L, 'A written statement of facts that the person swears is true, signed before a notary or other authorized officer.'),
('Attorney-Client Privilege', L, 'The legal protection that keeps confidential communications between a lawyer and a client from being disclosed to others.'),
('Work Product', L, 'Notes, research, strategy and other materials a lawyer prepares for a case. They are generally protected from disclosure to the other side.'),
('Retainer Agreement', L, 'The written contract between a client and a law firm that sets out the work to be done, the fee and each side’s responsibilities.'),
('Contingency Fee', L, 'A fee the lawyer is paid only if the case is won or settled, usually a percentage of the money recovered. Common in personal injury cases.'),
('Hourly Rate', L, 'A fee charged for each hour of the lawyer’s or staff’s time, rather than a percentage of a recovery.'),
('Power of Attorney (POA)', L, 'A document that lets one person act for another in legal or financial matters.'),
('Notary Public', L, 'A person authorized by the state to witness signatures, confirm identity and administer oaths.'),
('Statute', L, 'A written law passed by a legislature.'),
('Statute of Limitations (SOL)', L, 'The deadline set by law for filing a lawsuit. If it is missed, the claim is usually barred, however strong it is. The length depends on the state and the type of claim.'),
('Tolling', L, 'Pausing or extending a deadline, such as the statute of limitations, for a reason the law recognizes (for example, the claimant is a minor).'),
('Common Law', L, 'Law that comes from court decisions rather than statutes.'),
('Precedent', L, 'An earlier court decision that courts follow when deciding similar cases. Also called stare decisis, “to stand by things decided”.'),
('Appeal', L, 'Asking a higher court to review a lower court’s decision for legal error. The party who appeals is the appellant; the other side is the appellee.'),
('Remand', L, 'When a higher court sends a case back to a lower court for further action. Also used when a case moved to federal court is sent back to state court.'),
('Pro Se', L, 'Representing yourself in court without a lawyer.'),
('Pro Bono', L, 'Legal work done for free, for the public good.'),
('Pro Hac Vice', L, 'Latin for “for this occasion”. A court’s permission for a lawyer licensed in another state to handle one case.'),
('Of Counsel', L, 'A lawyer who is affiliated with a firm but is not a partner or regular associate.'),
('Opposing Counsel', L, 'The lawyer who represents the other side in a case.'),
('Paralegal', L, 'A trained professional who helps lawyers with legal work (drafting, research, case organization) but is not a lawyer and cannot give legal advice.'),
('Esquire (Esq.)', L, 'A courtesy title written after a lawyer’s name.'),
('State Bar', L, 'The organization that licenses and disciplines lawyers in a state. A lawyer is “admitted to the bar” of each state where they may practice.'),
('Conflict of Interest', L, 'A situation where a lawyer’s duty to one client, or their own interests, could harm another client. Firms run a conflict check before taking a new matter.'),
('Fiduciary Duty', L, 'The legal duty to act in another person’s best interest and with loyalty. A lawyer owes it to the client.'),
('Legal Malpractice', L, 'When a lawyer’s failure to meet professional standards, such as missing a deadline, harms a client.'),
('IOLTA / Trust Account', L, 'A bank account where a law firm holds client money, such as settlement funds, separate from the firm’s own money. Money is paid out only as the rules allow.'),
('Disbursement', L, 'Paying out money from a settlement: fees, costs, liens and bills first, then the net to the client.'),
('Settlement Statement (Closing Statement)', L, 'The final accounting of a settlement showing the total, attorney fees, costs, liens and bills paid, and the amount the client receives.'),
('Release', L, 'A document signed in a settlement in which the claimant gives up the right to sue over the claim in return for payment.'),
('Settlement', L, 'An agreement that resolves a dispute, usually for a payment, without a trial.'),
('Dismissal With Prejudice', L, 'The case is ended for good and cannot be filed again.'),
('Dismissal Without Prejudice', L, 'The case is ended but can be filed again, if the deadline allows.'),
('Judgment', L, 'The court’s final decision in a case, including any amount owed.'),
('Default Judgment', L, 'A judgment for the plaintiff because the defendant did not respond to the lawsuit in time.'),
('Lien', L, 'A legal claim on money or property as security for a debt. In injury cases, a lien lets a provider or insurer be repaid from the settlement.'),
('Garnishment', L, 'A court-ordered way to collect a debt by taking money from wages or a bank account.'),
('Subject-Matter Jurisdiction', L, 'A court’s authority to hear a certain type of case (for example, a bankruptcy court hears bankruptcy cases).'),
('Personal Jurisdiction', L, 'A court’s authority over the people or companies being sued, usually because they live, work or did business in that state.'),
('Diversity Jurisdiction', L, 'A federal court’s power to hear a state-law case when the parties are from different states and a minimum amount (over $75,000) is at stake.'),
('Federal Question', L, 'A case that arises under the US Constitution or a federal law, so a federal court can hear it.'),
('Hearsay', L, 'An out-of-court statement offered to prove that what it says is true. It is usually not allowed as evidence unless an exception applies.'),
('Expert Witness', L, 'A person with special training, such as a doctor or an engineer, who gives an opinion to help the judge or jury understand the evidence.'),
('Burden of Proof', L, 'The duty to prove a claim. In most civil cases the plaintiff has it.'),
('Preponderance of the Evidence', L, 'The usual civil standard: it is more likely than not (more than 50%) that the claim is true.'),
('Beyond a Reasonable Doubt', L, 'The criminal standard: the proof must leave no reasonable doubt that the defendant is guilty.'),
('Clear and Convincing Evidence', L, 'A standard higher than a preponderance of the evidence and lower than beyond a reasonable doubt, used in some civil matters.'),
('Admissible', L, 'Evidence that the judge allows the jury to consider.'),
('Objection', L, 'A lawyer’s formal protest that a question or evidence is improper. The judge rules “sustained” (agrees) or “overruled” (disagrees).'),
('Ex Parte', L, 'Done by or for one side only, without the other side being present or notified, as in an emergency request to the court.'),
('In Camera', L, 'Reviewed by the judge privately, in chambers, rather than in open court.'),
('Stipulation', L, 'A written agreement between the parties about a fact or a procedure, often filed with the court.'),
# ---- Torts and personal injury
('Tort', P, 'A civil wrong, other than breach of contract, that harms someone and for which the law allows a lawsuit for damages.'),
('Tortfeasor', P, 'The person or company that caused the harm. In a car accident, the at-fault driver.'),
('Negligence', P, 'Failing to use the care a reasonably careful person would use, which causes harm. It has four parts: duty, breach, causation and damages.'),
('Duty of Care', P, 'The legal obligation to act with reasonable care to avoid harming others.'),
('Breach of Duty', P, 'Failing to meet the duty of care, for example by speeding or ignoring a hazard.'),
('Proximate Cause', P, 'A cause that is close enough to the harm that the law holds the person responsible for it. The harm must have been a foreseeable result.'),
('Causation', P, 'The link between what the defendant did and the plaintiff’s injury. The plaintiff must show the injury was caused by the accident.'),
('Damages', P, 'The money a court or insurer awards to make up for a loss or injury.'),
('Economic Damages (Special Damages)', P, 'Losses that can be added up in dollars, such as medical bills, lost wages and property damage.'),
('Non-Economic Damages (General Damages)', P, 'Losses with no bill attached, such as pain and suffering, emotional distress and loss of enjoyment of life.'),
('Pain and Suffering', P, 'Compensation for physical pain and emotional distress caused by an injury.'),
('Punitive Damages', P, 'Money awarded to punish especially reckless or malicious conduct and to discourage it, over and above the loss itself. Allowed only in some cases.'),
('Compensatory Damages', P, 'Money that pays for the actual loss: medical bills, lost income, pain and suffering.'),
('Nominal Damages', P, 'A very small amount awarded when a right was violated but there is little or no financial loss.'),
('Loss of Consortium', P, 'A spouse’s claim for the loss of companionship, support and intimacy caused by the other spouse’s injury.'),
('Wrongful Death', P, 'A claim brought by certain family members or the estate when someone dies because of another’s negligence or wrongdoing.'),
('Premises Liability', P, 'A property owner’s or occupier’s responsibility for injuries caused by unsafe conditions on the property.'),
('Slip and Fall', P, 'A premises liability injury caused by a fall on a wet floor, uneven surface or other hazard.'),
('Product Liability', P, 'A manufacturer’s or seller’s responsibility for injuries caused by a defective or dangerous product.'),
('Medical Malpractice', P, 'Injury caused when a health care provider’s care falls below the accepted standard.'),
('Strict Liability', P, 'Responsibility for harm even without negligence or intent, as with some defective products or dangerous activities.'),
('Vicarious Liability', P, 'Responsibility for another person’s wrongdoing because of a relationship, such as an employer for an employee. Also called respondeat superior.'),
('Comparative Negligence', P, 'The rule that reduces a plaintiff’s recovery by their own percentage of fault. In “pure” systems they can recover even if mostly at fault; in “modified” systems they are barred above 50% or 51%.'),
('Contributory Negligence', P, 'A rule in a few states that bars recovery entirely if the injured person was even slightly at fault.'),
('Assumption of Risk', P, 'A defense that the injured person knew about a danger and accepted the risk anyway.'),
('Mitigation of Damages', P, 'The injured person’s duty to take reasonable steps to limit their losses, such as following medical advice.'),
('Eggshell Plaintiff (Thin Skull Rule)', P, 'The defendant takes the injured person as they find them, so is responsible for the full harm even if the person was unusually fragile.'),
('Aggravation of a Pre-existing Condition', P, 'Making an earlier injury or condition worse. The defendant may be responsible for the worsening, not the original condition.'),
('Treatment Gap', P, 'A period with no medical treatment between visits. Adjusters may argue the injury was not serious, so gaps should be explained.'),
('MIST (Minor Impact Soft Tissue)', P, 'The label insurers put on low-damage collisions with soft-tissue injuries, used to argue the crash could not have caused the injury.'),
('Soft Tissue Injury', P, 'An injury to muscles, ligaments or tendons, such as whiplash, that often does not show on X-rays.'),
('Whiplash', P, 'A neck injury caused by a sudden back-and-forth movement of the head, common in rear-end collisions.'),
('Demand Letter', P, 'A letter to the insurance company that sets out the facts, the injuries, the bills and a dollar amount requested to settle.'),
('Demand Package', P, 'The demand letter plus its supporting records: medical records, bills, wage loss proof and photos.'),
('Pre-Suit', P, 'The stage before a lawsuit is filed, when the claim is handled directly with the insurance company.'),
('Statute of Repose', P, 'A fixed outer deadline for bringing certain claims, such as defective products or construction, counted from an event rather than from the injury.'),
('Mass Tort', P, 'A group of many people injured by the same product, drug or event, each with their own claim.'),
('Class Action', P, 'A lawsuit brought by one or a few people on behalf of a large group with similar claims.'),
('Multidistrict Litigation (MDL)', P, 'A federal procedure that combines many similar cases from different districts before one judge for pretrial work.'),
# ---- Insurance and liens
('Adjuster', I, 'The insurance company employee or contractor who investigates a claim and decides or recommends what to pay.'),
('Claimant', I, 'The person making an insurance claim.'),
('Insured', I, 'The person or business covered by an insurance policy.'),
('Policy Limits', I, 'The most an insurance policy will pay for a covered claim, such as $25,000 per person and $50,000 per accident.'),
('Declarations Page (Dec Page)', I, 'The first page of an insurance policy, listing who is insured, the vehicles, the coverages and the limits.'),
('Bodily Injury (BI)', I, 'Coverage for injuries a policyholder causes to other people.'),
('Property Damage (PD)', I, 'Coverage for damage a policyholder causes to someone else’s property, such as their car.'),
('First-Party Claim', I, 'A claim made against your own insurance company.'),
('Third-Party Claim', I, 'A claim made against the at-fault person’s insurance company.'),
('UM / UIM (Uninsured / Underinsured Motorist)', I, 'Coverage on your own policy that pays when the at-fault driver has no insurance, or not enough.'),
('PIP (Personal Injury Protection)', I, 'No-fault coverage that pays your medical bills and some lost income after a crash, whoever was at fault, in states that require it.'),
('MedPay (Medical Payments Coverage)', I, 'Optional coverage that pays medical bills after a crash regardless of fault, up to a set limit.'),
('No-Fault', I, 'A system where your own insurance pays certain crash losses, such as medical bills, whoever caused the crash. Lawsuits are limited to more serious injuries.'),
('Excess / Umbrella Policy', I, 'Extra liability coverage that applies after the main policy limits are used up.'),
('Deductible', I, 'The amount you pay yourself before the insurance pays the rest.'),
('Premium', I, 'The amount paid to keep an insurance policy in force.'),
('Letter of Representation (LOR)', I, 'A letter telling an insurance company that a law firm represents the claimant, so they should contact the firm and not the claimant.'),
('Letter of Protection (LOP)', I, 'A written promise from a law firm or client to pay a provider’s bill from the settlement, so the client can treat now and pay later.'),
('Total Loss', I, 'When the cost to repair a vehicle is more than a set share of its value, so the insurer pays its value instead.'),
('Diminished Value', I, 'The loss in a vehicle’s market value after an accident, even when repaired.'),
('Loss of Use', I, 'Compensation for the time a vehicle is out of service, such as the cost of a rental car.'),
('Subrogation', I, 'The right of an insurer or plan that paid a claim to be repaid from the person who caused the loss, or from the injured person’s settlement.'),
('Medical Lien', I, 'A claim by a medical provider on a settlement for unpaid treatment.'),
('Hospital Lien', I, 'A claim by a hospital on an injury settlement, created by a state statute or by contract, for the cost of emergency or hospital care.'),
('Attorney’s Lien', I, 'A lawyer’s legal claim on a recovery for unpaid fees and costs.'),
('Medicare Conditional Payment', I, 'Money Medicare paid for accident-related care while a claim was pending. Medicare must be repaid from the settlement.'),
('Medicaid Lien', I, 'The state Medicaid program’s claim to be repaid from a settlement for accident-related care it paid for.'),
('ERISA Plan', I, 'A health plan from a private employer governed by a federal law. A self-funded ERISA plan can claim strong repayment rights from a settlement, subject to its plan terms.'),
('Made-Whole Doctrine', I, 'The rule that an injured person must be fully compensated before an insurer can take repayment from the settlement. Plan terms and state law can change it.'),
('Common Fund Doctrine', I, 'The rule that a lienholder who benefits from a lawyer’s work should help pay the attorney fees and costs, which reduces the lien.'),
('Lien Reduction', I, 'Negotiating a lienholder down to a smaller repayment from a settlement.'),
('Net to Client', I, 'The money the client receives after fees, costs, liens and bills are paid.'),
('Explanation of Benefits (EOB)', I, 'A statement from a health insurer showing what was billed, what it paid and what the patient owes.'),
# ---- Litigation
('Complaint', T, 'The document that starts a lawsuit. It names the parties, states the facts and says what the plaintiff wants. In some states it is called a petition.'),
('Summons', T, 'A court document that tells a defendant they are being sued and how long they have to respond.'),
('Answer', T, 'The defendant’s written response to the complaint, admitting or denying each claim and raising defenses.'),
('Counterclaim', T, 'A claim the defendant makes against the plaintiff in the same lawsuit.'),
('Cross-Claim', T, 'A claim one defendant makes against another defendant in the same lawsuit.'),
('Third-Party Complaint', T, 'A claim a defendant makes against a new party who may be responsible for some or all of the harm.'),
('Pleading', T, 'A formal document that states a party’s claims or defenses, such as a complaint or an answer.'),
('Caption', T, 'The heading of a court document that gives the court, the parties’ names and the case number.'),
('Case Number', T, 'The number the court assigns to a case, used to find and file anything in it.'),
('Docket', T, 'The court’s official list of everything filed and every event in a case. To “docket” something is also to enter a deadline on the firm’s calendar.'),
('Motion', T, 'A written request asking the court to make a ruling or order.'),
('Motion to Dismiss', T, 'A request to end the case early, arguing the complaint fails to state a valid claim or the court lacks authority.'),
('Demurrer', T, 'In some states, such as California, a challenge to the complaint saying that even if the facts are true there is no valid claim.'),
('Motion for Summary Judgment', T, 'A request for a decision without a trial because there is no real dispute about the important facts.'),
('Motion in Limine', T, 'A request, usually before trial, to allow or keep out certain evidence.'),
('Motion to Compel', T, 'A request that the court order the other side to answer discovery or produce documents.'),
('Discovery', T, 'The stage where each side gets information from the other through written questions, document requests and depositions.'),
('Interrogatories', T, 'Written questions one side sends the other that must be answered in writing under oath.'),
('Request for Production (RFP)', T, 'A written request that the other side hand over documents or things.'),
('Request for Admission (RFA)', T, 'A written request that the other side admit or deny that a fact is true. An admitted fact is settled for the case.'),
('Deposition', T, 'Out-of-court testimony given under oath, answering a lawyer’s questions, and recorded by a court reporter.'),
('Subpoena', T, 'A court order requiring a person to testify or to hand over documents.'),
('Subpoena Duces Tecum', T, 'A subpoena that orders someone to bring documents or things, such as medical or billing records.'),
('Protective Order', T, 'A court order that limits how information from discovery can be shared or used.'),
('Privilege Log', T, 'A list of documents withheld from discovery as privileged, with enough detail to explain why.'),
('Spoliation', T, 'Destroying, losing or altering evidence that should have been kept for a case.'),
('Litigation Hold (Preservation Letter)', T, 'A notice telling a person or company to keep all records and evidence related to a claim.'),
('Service of Process', T, 'Formally delivering the summons and complaint to the defendant so they have legal notice of the lawsuit.'),
('Process Server', T, 'A person whose job is to deliver legal papers to the people being sued.'),
('Proof of Service', T, 'A signed statement filed with the court that shows when, where and how the papers were served.'),
('Scheduling Order', T, 'The court’s order setting the deadlines for a case, such as discovery cut-off, motions and trial date.'),
('Continuance', T, 'A postponement of a hearing or trial to a later date.'),
('Stay', T, 'A court order that pauses a case or part of it.'),
('Injunction', T, 'A court order that requires someone to do, or stop doing, something. A temporary restraining order (TRO) is a short-term emergency version.'),
('Sanctions', T, 'Penalties a court gives for breaking its rules, such as ignoring discovery requests.'),
('Meet and Confer', T, 'A required step where the lawyers must try to resolve a dispute, often about discovery, with each other before asking the court.'),
('Mediation', T, 'A meeting where a neutral person, the mediator, helps the parties negotiate a settlement. The mediator cannot force a result.'),
('Arbitration', T, 'A private process where a neutral arbitrator hears both sides and decides the case. It can be binding or non-binding.'),
('Settlement Conference', T, 'A meeting, often led by a judge or another neutral, to try to settle a case before trial.'),
('Mediation Binder', T, 'A package of key documents, such as the facts, injuries, bills and demand, prepared for a mediation.'),
('Offer of Judgment', T, 'A formal settlement offer under a court rule that can shift costs to a party who rejects it and then does worse at trial.'),
('Voir Dire', T, 'The questioning of possible jurors to choose a fair jury.'),
('Jury Instructions', T, 'The judge’s explanation to the jury of the law it must apply to the facts.'),
('Verdict', T, 'The jury’s (or the judge’s) decision on the facts of the case.'),
('Directed Verdict', T, 'A ruling by the judge during trial that one side wins because no reasonable jury could decide otherwise. Now often called judgment as a matter of law.'),
('Exhibit', T, 'A document, photo or object offered as evidence, or attached to a court filing, and labelled by number or letter.'),
('Bates Number', T, 'A unique number stamped on each page of documents in a case so each page can be identified.'),
('Joinder', T, 'Adding another party or claim to a lawsuit.'),
('Removal', T, 'Moving a case from state court to federal court, which is allowed only in certain cases.'),
('E-Filing', T, 'Filing court documents online through the court’s electronic system.'),
('PACER', T, 'The federal courts’ online service for searching and downloading case documents and dockets.'),
('Court Reporter', T, 'The person who records and transcribes testimony at depositions and court proceedings.'),
('Transcript', T, 'A word-for-word written record of testimony or a court proceeding.'),
('Writ', T, 'A written court order commanding someone to do or not do something.'),
('Remittitur', T, 'A judge’s reduction of a jury’s award that the judge finds too high.'),
# ---- Medical-legal terms used in injury cases
('HIPAA', M, 'The federal privacy law that protects patients’ medical information. Providers need a signed authorization before releasing records to a law firm.'),
('HIPAA Authorization', M, 'A form signed by the patient allowing a provider to release their medical records to a named person or firm.'),
('Medical Records Custodian', M, 'The person or department at a provider who handles records requests and can certify the records.'),
('Itemized Bill', M, 'A provider’s bill that lists each service, date and charge, rather than only a total.'),
('Medical Chronology', M, 'A date-by-date summary of a person’s treatment, built from their medical records.'),
('Medical Summary', M, 'A written summary of the injuries, treatment, findings and costs, used in a demand.'),
('CPT Code', M, 'A five-digit code that identifies each medical service or procedure on a bill.'),
('ICD-10 Code', M, 'A code that identifies the diagnosis (the condition or injury) on medical records and bills.'),
('CMS-1500 (HCFA)', M, 'The standard billing form used by doctors and most outpatient providers to bill insurers.'),
('UB-04', M, 'The standard billing form hospitals and facilities use to bill insurers.'),
('Independent Medical Examination (IME)', M, 'An exam by a doctor chosen by the insurance company or defense, to give an opinion on the injury and treatment.'),
('Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI)', M, 'The point when a person’s condition has stabilized and is not expected to improve much more with treatment.'),
('Impairment Rating', M, 'A doctor’s estimate, as a percentage, of the permanent loss of function from an injury.'),
('Usual and Customary Charges', M, 'The typical amount providers in an area charge for a service, used to judge whether a bill is reasonable.'),
('Write-Off (Adjustment)', M, 'The part of a bill a provider does not collect because of an insurance contract or a negotiated reduction.'),
('Future Medical Care', M, 'Treatment a doctor expects a person will need later, such as surgery, which can be claimed as damages if supported.'),
]
